mode systems

# digest.tw: SHA-256, over I64.
#
# An archive that carries a content hash and computes it with a
# non-cryptographic function carries a checksum wearing a hash's name. FNV or a
# CRC detects a flipped bit on a disk; it does not detect substitution, and
# substitution is the threat a model registry has. A model file is a program in
# every sense that matters, and the reason to hash it is so that "the weights I
# evaluated" and "the weights I deployed" can be shown to be the same weights.
#
# ---- this is the second copy in the ecosystem, and that is a bug -----------
#
# spool has `src/sha256.tw`. This is not it, and it should be. Neither
# repository can import the other's: twill resolves a non-`std/` import as a
# path relative to the importing file, so only `std/` modules are reachable from
# an installed package, and spool is an application rather than a package.
#
# Two SHA-256 implementations in one ecosystem drift, and the drift is invisible
# until two tools disagree about a hash and neither is obviously wrong. The fix
# is `std/hash`, at which point this file is deleted. docs/needs.md entry 7.
# Duplicating it was the lesser of two bad options; the worse one was to
# reference spool's file across a repository boundary that the import rule
# cannot express.
#
# ---- the cost, stated ------------------------------------------------------
#
# This runs the compression function in a tree-walking interpreter, one 32-bit
# word at a time, on a value type that is 64 bits wide. It will be slow, on the
# order of a megabyte per second. Hashing a 12 kB parameter tree is nothing;
# hashing a 400 MB archive at that rate is several minutes and is not
# acceptable, which is why `docs/needs.md` entry 7 asks for a builtin rather
# than for `std/hash` alone. selvedge hashes the payload once on write and once
# on an explicit verify, never on an ordinary load, precisely because of this.
#
# Every intermediate is masked back to 32 bits after every step, because I64
# wraps at 64 bits and SHA-256 wraps at 32.

# The compression function itself now lives in twill's standard library, as
# `std/hash`. This module keeps its name and its two exports so that every call
# site is unchanged, and keeps the argument above for why the digest has to be a
# real one; what it no longer keeps is a second hand-written SHA-256. Two copies
# of this algorithm in one ecosystem is one copy too many -- selvedge's
# docs/needs.md asked for exactly this ("std/hash, and a SHA-256 that is not
# interpreted"), and the copy that was here disagreed with the reference on
# every input, because `not(x)` is the boolean negation and the algorithm wants
# the bitwise one.

import "std/hash" as sha
import "bytes_compat.tw" as b

# The lowercase hex SHA-256 of a string's bytes.
fn hash(msg: Str) -> Str = sha.hash_str(msg)

# The first `n` hex characters of a digest, for display. Never for comparison:
# a registry that matched on a prefix would be a registry with a collision
# surface it chose for itself.
fn short(d: Str, n: I64) -> Str = b.slice(d, 0, n)
